        "text": "Subsecciones de: Chapter 14 Messing with Video and Graphics\nP\u00e1ginas: 289-306\n\u2022 Watchin\u2019 Videos on the Web  (289)\n  \u2022 Firefox video support  (290)\n  \u2022 Getting even more video support  (291)\n\u2022 Viewing Movie Files  (293)\n  \u2022 GNOME Videos  (294)\n  \u2022 KDE Dragon  (295)\n  \u2022 VLC  (296)\n\u2022 Watchin\u2019 DVDs  (297)\n\u2022 Creating and Modifying Graphics  (298)\n  \u2022 GIMP basic tools  (299)\n  \u2022 Capturing screenshots  (300)\n  \u2022 Editing an image file  (301)\n  \u2022 Using GIMP scripts  (303)\n\u2022 Playing 3D Games  (304)\nChapter 14\nMessing with Video and\nGraphics\nT\nhere\u2019s no question that video is one area where Linux has been playing\ncatch-up. The original Linux systems didn\u2019t even support a Windows-style\ndesktop! In the early days of Linux, it was close to impossible to display any\ntype of videos, let alone use them for playing movies or high-end computer\ngraphics games.\nFortunately, nowadays, things are much different. There aren\u2019t many things your\nLinux computer can\u2019t do in the video world (unless of course it\u2019s illegal, but more\non that later). In this chapter, I walk you through your Linux computer\u2019s video\nand graphics capabilities.",
